# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
  "base": [
    ("Flatwoods Water Tables Under a Stamped Pool Deck",
     "<p>A stamped pool deck sits close to grade, and across much of Greater Orlando and the Suncoast the ground underneath is flatwoods soil, Myakka, Smyrna or EauGallie among them, with a water table that can sit within 18 inches of the surface for a stretch of most years. That is workable ground, but only once the saturated layer is identified and removed rather than assumed away because the pool itself is nearby. A {city} pool deck poured over soggy native soil without compacted fill underneath settles unevenly around the shell faster than the rest of the yard, since the shell itself does not move the way the surrounding slab can.</p>"),
    ("Ridge Sand Needs Wetting Before It Will Compact for a Driveway",
     "<p>West of Orlando, sand ridge soils such as Candler and Astatula drain so well that the water table can sit more than 80 inches down, which sounds like easy ground but loose, excessively drained sand will not lock together under a plate compactor the way firmer soil does. For a stamped driveway on a ridge lot in {city}, we wet the base material enough to bind it and compact it in shallow lifts rather than one deep pass. Skipping that step on a decorative driveway is a costlier mistake than on a plain one, since replacing a sunken panel means matching a color and pattern that has already weathered a season or two.</p>"),
    ("The Base Layer Doesn't Change Because the Finish Is Decorative",
     "<p>Florida's residential code calls for at least 4 inches of clean graded sand, gravel or crushed stone under a slab on the ground, set over fill compacted for uniform support, and that requirement reads the same whether the surface on top ends up plain gray or a stamped pattern. A {city} homeowner comparing quotes sometimes assumes a stamped driveway or patio comes with an upgraded base to match the upgraded finish, but the prep work underneath is the same code minimum either way. What the extra cost of stamping actually pays for is color, texture and the labor of pressing and detailing the pattern, not a different subgrade.</p>"),
    ("Why a Stamped Pool Deck Needs an Isolation Joint at the Shell",
     "<p>A pool shell and the deck around it move independently, expanding and settling at different rates, which is why the slab is isolated from the coping with a joint that lets each side shift without cracking the other. On a stamped {city} pool deck, that isolation joint has to be planned into the pattern layout before the first mat goes down, since cutting it in afterward would cross through the middle of a repeating design instead of following a seam. Skipping the isolation joint, or hiding it under grout-colored filler instead of a proper joint material, is one of the more common reasons a stamped deck cracks at the coping line within the first year or two.</p>"),
    ("Grading a Stamped Patio Away From the House, Not Just the Pool",
     "<p>A stamped patio that slopes toward the house instead of away from it sends every afternoon storm back at the foundation, and on a patio that wraps around a pool, it is easy to grade carefully toward the deck drains while forgetting the stretch that runs along the house wall. Florida's compaction standard calls for fill shaped for uniform support, and part of getting that right on a {city} patio is setting the subgrade slope before the pattern work starts, since a stamped surface cannot be re-sloped later without tearing it out. This matters most on flatwoods lots where the water table already sits close to the surface for months at a time.</p>"),
    ("Base Depth Doesn't Shrink Just Because the Pour Is a Walkway",
     "<p>A stamped walkway connecting a driveway to the front door is often the smallest pour on a lot, and that size tempts a shortcut on prep work that a driveway or patio would never get. Florida's base-material requirement, at least 4 inches of clean graded sand, gravel or crushed stone set over compacted fill, does not scale down for a narrower run of concrete. On a {city} walkway crossing a planting bed or an old sprinkler trench, skipping that base layer because the path looks minor is how a 3-foot-wide ribbon of stamped concrete ends up with the same settling and cracking problems as a driveway ten times its size, just sooner.</p>"),
    ("A Stamped Pattern Shows What a Broom Finish Hides",
     "<p>Florida's residential code requires fill under a slab to be compacted for uniform support, not simply leveled by eye, and that standard matters more under a stamped surface than under a plain one. A broom-finished driveway can carry a shallow low spot in the base without anyone noticing, because the texture and the lack of pattern lines make a quarter-inch dip hard to see. The same dip under a {city} stamped pattern breaks the straight grout lines running across the slab, so the base has to be checked and compacted in lifts rather than assumed even, because the surface on top is about to hide it either way.</p>"),
    ("What's Already Buried Before the Base Goes In",
     "<p>A patio or pool deck footprint often overlaps an existing irrigation line, a low-voltage landscape wire or a drain pipe that was never meant to sit under a slab, and none of that shows up until the base excavation starts. On a {city} job, we locate and either cap, reroute or sleeve anything buried in the footprint before compacted fill goes down, because once a stamped surface is poured over it, a leak or a dead wire is no longer a five-minute fix. Rerouting costs real time at the base stage, but it is far less than cutting into a finished stamped driveway or patio later to chase the same problem.</p>"),
  ],
  "build": [
    ("Four Inches Is the Driveway Standard, Decorative or Not",
     "<p>A stamped driveway built for daily passenger traffic follows the same thickness rule as a plain one: 4 inches at 3,000 to 4,000 psi, with Florida's code minimum of 3 1/2 inches written for ordinary floors rather than a surface carrying a car's weight every day. Thickness does not change because the surface will be stamped and colored. What changes is the price of getting it wrong: thickening a plain driveway later means breaking out and repouring gray concrete, while fixing an undersized stamped {city} driveway means matching a pattern, a color and however much the sealer has already weathered before the repair crew shows up.</p>"),
    ("Integral Color Goes Into the Mix, Color Hardener Goes on Top",
     "<p>Integral color is added to the concrete at the plant or the truck, tinting the entire thickness of the slab, which means a chip or a wear spot shows the same color underneath rather than bare gray. Color hardener is broadcast onto the surface by hand after the pour, floated in, and only penetrates the top layer, which produces a harder, more saturated surface but leaves plain concrete underneath if that layer ever wears through. A {city} homeowner picking between the two is really choosing between a subtler, more uniform tone from integral color and a deeper, more dramatic color range available with hardener.</p>"),
    ("What the Release Agent on a Stamped Surface Is Actually For",
     "<p>A powder or liquid release agent is dusted or sprayed over the surface right before the stamp mats go down, keeping the textured mats from sticking to the wet concrete and, as a side effect, leaving a secondary color in the low points of the pattern that reads as an antiqued or weathered look once it is pressed in. The residue has to be pressure washed off within the days after stamping and before sealing, because leaving it in place tracks a chalky film onto shoes, pool decks and anything else it touches. On a {city} pour, skipping the release step entirely is not really an option, since bare mats on wet concrete would tear the surface apart when lifted.</p>"),
    ("Control Joints Still Exist, They're Just Hidden in the Pattern",
     "<p>A stamped surface does not get a pass on control joints; the same spacing guidance that applies to plain concrete, roughly 24 to 36 times the slab's thickness, still governs a stamped driveway or patio. What changes is where the joint goes: rather than cutting a visible groove across a decorative surface, the layout is planned so a joint falls along a grout line already built into the pattern, usually where one course or color band meets another. On a {city} job with an irregular layout, an L-shaped patio wrapping a pool equipment pad for instance, that planning happens before the first mat is pressed, since a joint added afterward would cut straight through the pattern instead of hiding in it.</p>"),
    ("Why Fiber Often Replaces Wire Mesh Under a Stamp Mat",
     "<p>Wire mesh has to be pulled up into the middle of the slab as the pour goes in, and a crew pressing heavy rubber or polyurethane stamp mats into wet concrete can push mesh that was not positioned well right back down to the bottom, where it does little good. Synthetic fiber mixed into the batch at the plant disperses through the whole slab instead, so there is nothing to hold in place or push out of position while the mats are being worked. For a stamped driveway or pool deck in {city}, fiber reinforcement fits the stamping process better, even though, like mesh, it keeps a crack from opening rather than stopping one from forming in the first place.</p>"),
    ("A Flagstone Pattern and an Ashlar Slate Pattern Don't Lay Out the Same",
     "<p>An irregular flagstone pattern has wide, randomly spaced grout lines that can double as the slab's control joints without much extra planning, while a tight ashlar slate or running-bond brick pattern packs its lines closer together than the spacing a control joint actually needs. On a {city} driveway using a tighter pattern, we still saw-cut true control joints at the proper interval, choosing cuts that fall along an existing pattern line wherever the layout allows it. Picking a pattern is partly an aesthetic decision, but the grid it creates on the surface also decides how much extra joint planning the job needs before the first stamp goes down.</p>"),
    ("Sealer Grit Is a Spec Decision Around a Pool, Not an Afterthought",
     "<p>A stamped pool deck gets wet in a way a driveway rarely does, and a sealer with a slip-resistant additive mixed in, usually a fine polymer grit, has to be part of the spec for that surface rather than chosen later. A driveway or front walkway in {city} can often get by with a standard sealer, but the same product on a wet pool deck turns a textured stamped surface slick underfoot even though the pattern itself still has grip built into it. Deciding on grit at the sealing stage, not after the first slippery afternoon by the pool, is the difference between a textured deck that performs as designed and one that only looks like it does.</p>"),
    ("A Border Band Is Still a Real Joint, Just a Decorative One",
     "<p>An accent border in a contrasting color or pattern, framing a field of flagstone or running bond, is laid out along what has to function as a true joint between the border and the field, not just a visual line. That seam carries the same job a saw-cut control joint would: letting the two sections move slightly without cracking into each other. On a {city} patio or driveway with a border, we plan that joint's position the same way we would any other, by slab thickness and panel shape, then let the color and pattern change do double duty as the joint's cosmetic cover. Skipping that planning is how a border cracks straight through instead of along its own seam.</p>"),
  ],
  "process": [
    ("The Order of Operations Between the Pour and the Stamp",
     "<p>A stamped slab goes through more finishing steps than a plain one before a broom or a trowel gets put away: screed and bull float first, then color hardener broadcast and floated in where it is used, then edging and the control joint layout, then a texture skin check to confirm the surface has firmed up enough to hold an imprint. Only after that does the crew press the stamp mats in, working panel by panel, followed by hand tools at borders and tight corners the mats cannot reach. On a {city} pour, rushing ahead to any one of those steps before the previous one is finished is the most common way a stamped job goes wrong.</p>"),
    ("Why the Stamping Window Closes Faster on a Hot Afternoon",
     "<p>Concrete has to stay plastic enough to take a clean imprint, and in hot, dry, windy conditions, the kind hot-weather concreting guidance flags once the evaporation rate climbs past roughly 0.2 pounds per square foot per hour, that window closes noticeably faster than it does on a mild, overcast day. A crew stamping a {city} driveway or patio in summer works in smaller sections and starts earlier in the day specifically to keep pace with the surface stiffening, not just to avoid the cracking risk that comes with it. Waiting too long between finishing and stamping on a hot pour means pressing mats into concrete that has already started to set, which blurs the imprint instead of sharpening it.</p>"),
    ("An Afternoon Storm Does More Damage Mid-Stamp Than Mid-Pour",
     "<p>Summer storms in this part of Florida tend to build through the morning and break loose by early afternoon, inside a wet season that generally stretches from late spring into mid-October, and timing matters more on a stamped job than a plain one because rain hitting the surface before the mats are lifted can blur the imprint and streak color hardener before either has set. On a {city} job scheduled during that stretch, finishing and stamping early enough to be done before a typical storm arrives is part of the plan, not a backup option. Outside the wettest months the schedule has more room, but the sequence between finishing and stamping still gets watched against the forecast either way.</p>"),
    ("The Day After: Washing Off Release and Seeing the Real Color",
     "<p>The release agent used to keep stamp mats from sticking leaves a chalky residue across the whole surface, and the color underneath does not show its true tone until that residue is pressure washed off, typically once the slab has firmed up enough to walk on without damaging the texture. On a {city} job, this cleanup happens as its own step, separate from the pour and the stamping day, and it is often the first time a homeowner sees what the finished color actually looks like rather than the dusty version visible right after stamping. Sealing comes later, once the slab has fully cured, not at this stage.</p>"),
    ("Sealing Waits for the Cure, Not Just a Dry Surface",
     "<p>Hot-weather guidance calls for curing to continue at least 3 days after finishing, whether through a curing compound or wet curing, and a stamped surface follows that same minimum before anything else happens to it. Sealing too early, before the cure period is finished and the slab has had time to dry out, can trap moisture under the sealer film and cloud the finish instead of saturating the color the way it should. On a {city} job, the gap between the stamping day and the sealing day is not wasted time: it is the window the slab needs to reach enough strength and dryness for the sealer, including any slip-resistant grit mixed into it, to bond properly.</p>"),
    ("Breaking a Large Pool Deck Into Sections the Crew Can Keep Up With",
     "<p>A big pool deck or a long stamped driveway can outrun a crew's working time if it is poured all at once, since the leading edge may already be stiffening by the time the trailing edge is screeded. Splitting the pour into sections, with a planned joint at each day's stopping point, lets the crew finish, broadcast hardener, texture and stamp one section fully before the concrete sets, rather than racing the whole slab against the clock. On a {city} job large enough to need more than one pour day, that section joint gets planned into the pattern layout in advance, so it reads as part of the design rather than an afterthought.</p>"),
    ("Removing an Old Pool Deck Without Damaging the Shell",
     "<p>Tearing out an existing pool deck to replace it with a stamped surface is slower and more careful work than demolishing a driveway, since the old slab sits right against the coping and the pool shell, and a misplaced strike with demolition equipment can crack the shell or chip the coping it is supposed to come out cleanly from. On a {city} deck replacement, that edge gets broken out by hand or with smaller equipment once the bulk of the field has been cleared by machine. Only after demolition is finished and the subgrade around the shell can actually be seen does the new base and isolation joint get planned against what is really there.</p>"),
    ("The Base Has to Pass Inspection Before the Color Goes Down",
     "<p>A permit inspection, when {city} or the county requires one, almost always happens at the forming and base stage, well before color hardener, release agent or stamp mats enter the picture, because none of that groundwork is visible once the surface is finished. On a stamped job, a delay at this point costs more than it would on a plain pour, since pushing the pour date back can also push the job out of the narrow weather window the crew planned to stamp in, particularly during the rainy season. Scheduling the inspection as early as the permit process allows protects not just the pour date but the sequence of finishing steps lined up behind it.</p>"),
  ],
  "risks": [
    ("A Crack That Forms Before Stamping Runs Straight Through the Pattern",
     "<p>Plastic shrinkage cracking shows up when the evaporation rate off a fresh slab climbs above roughly 0.2 pounds per square foot per hour, the kind of hot, breezy, low-humidity afternoon Florida produces often enough, and on a surface that has not been stamped yet, those thin parallel cracks run straight across whatever pattern eventually goes over them. A {city} pour on a day like that needs windbreaks, a fog spray or a dampened subgrade before the first mat is even brought out, because once a crack like this forms it cannot be stamped away, only patched in a way that rarely matches the surrounding color and texture. Watching conditions matters more here than on a job that will stay plain gray.</p>"),
    ("Rain on a Half-Stamped Surface Is Worse Than Rain on a Bare Slab",
     "<p>A downpour hitting plain concrete before it sets can wash out the surface finish, but the same storm hitting a slab mid-stamp, after color hardener is down and some mats are pressed in but others are not, can streak the color unevenly and blur the texture on whichever panels caught the worst of it. On a {city} job, that risk is why crews track the radar through the stamping window the same way they would through the pour itself, not just up to the point the truck leaves. Patching a rain-damaged section of a stamped surface afterward almost always leaves a visible seam where the repair meets the original color and pattern.</p>"),
    ("Flaking Color Hardener Is a Floating Problem, Not a Pattern Problem",
     "<p>Color hardener that is broadcast onto a surface too wet, or floated in before enough of it has absorbed moisture from the slab, can form a thin shell that never fully bonds to the concrete underneath, and that shell eventually flakes or spalls off under traffic, usually within the first year or two. It has nothing to do with which stamp pattern was used and everything to do with the floating technique and the timing of the broadcast relative to the slab's set. A {city} homeowner who notices small chips revealing gray concrete under a colored surface is looking at a hardener application problem, not a sign the whole pour needs to be replaced.</p>"),
    ("Why a Sealed Pool Deck's Color Fades Unevenly, Not All at Once",
     "<p>Sun and, around a pool, chlorinated splash both break down an acrylic sealer over time, and because a {city} deck rarely gets the same sun exposure or the same amount of pool water across its whole surface, the fading shows up unevenly instead of as one uniform shift in tone: a shaded corner can still look glossy while a sun-struck edge near the water has gone chalky. That unevenness is sealer wear, not a flaw in the concrete or the color underneath it, and it is corrected by stripping and recoating the surface rather than by anything done to the slab itself.</p>"),
    ("A Textured Surface Can Still Turn Slick Around Water",
     "<p>Stamping adds grip through its texture, but sealer applied without a slip-resistant additive mixed in can build up a smooth film over that texture, especially after a few recoats, and the result is a pool deck that looks rough but feels slick the moment it gets wet. This risk is specific to surfaces that see standing water regularly, a pool deck more than a driveway, and it is easy to miss because the pattern itself still looks like it should provide traction. On a {city} deck, checking for this before the next pool season, not after someone slips, is the only way to catch a sealer problem the texture underneath is hiding.</p>"),
    ("Deeper Pattern Grooves Hold Salt Air Closer to the Reinforcement",
     "<p>A stamped surface has more texture, and more grout-line depth, than a plain broom finish, which means that near the Gulf, where airborne salt speeds up corrosion of any exposed wire mesh or fiber-reinforced crack, a stamped driveway or patio can hold moisture and salt residue in its grooves longer than a smoother surface would after the same rain. If {city} sits in one of the coastal stretches of Sarasota or Manatee where salt air reaches the yard regularly, that texture is worth factoring into how often the surface gets rinsed and resealed, not just how it looks after installation. Inland, this risk mostly does not apply.</p>"),
    ("HOA Approval for a Stamped Driveway Can Cover Color, Not Just Permits",
     "<p>A homeowners association's authority to review the location, size, type or appearance of a project comes from its declaration or published guidelines, and where that authority reasonably covers appearance, a stamped pattern or color choice can fall under architectural review even on a lot where the permit itself is straightforward. A {city} homeowner who assumes a cleared building permit means the driveway or patio design is settled can still run into a separate HOA objection over the color or pattern selected, since the two approvals cover different things. Checking both before ordering materials avoids a stamp pattern that passes inspection but gets flagged by the association afterward.</p>"),
    ("Why Fine Surface Cracking Shows Up More on a Colored Slab",
     "<p>Crazing, the web of hairline surface cracks that can form when a slab's surface dries faster than hot-weather guidance recommends, is largely cosmetic and does not weaken a plain gray driveway much, but the same crazing on an integrally colored or hardener-finished surface catches light differently than the surrounding concrete and reads as a network of pale lines against the color. A {city} pour finished on a hot, high-evaporation day without the usual mitigations, fog spray, a dampened subgrade, an evaporation retarder, risks crazing that would barely register on plain concrete but stands out clearly once the color and sealer are on.</p>"),
  ],
  "care": [
    ("Reading Wear Instead of a Calendar Before Resealing",
     "<p>No fixed interval sets when a stamped surface needs its sealer reapplied; UV exposure, foot and tire traffic, and for a pool deck, splashed pool chemicals all wear a sealer down at different rates depending on sun and use. A {city} homeowner is better off watching for the signs, a duller sheen, color that looks chalky rather than saturated, water that no longer beads on the surface, than waiting for a specific number of years to pass. Resealing before those signs turn into actual wear on the concrete underneath keeps the color protected and costs far less than letting the surface go bare.</p>"),
    ("Why a Stamped Surface Needs a Wider Fan Tip Than a Driveway",
     "<p>The grooves in a stamped pattern run deeper than the texture on a broom-finished driveway, which means dirt, pollen and organic growth settle into them more readily, especially in Florida's humidity. Pressure washing a {city} patio or pool deck still works, but a narrow, concentrated nozzle that would be fine on plain concrete can etch the sealer or strip color right out of a stamped surface's low points. A wider fan tip held at a steady distance clears the grooves without cutting into the finish, and it is worth testing on a small corner before running the same setting across the whole surface.</p>"),
    ("Clogged Pattern Lines Trap Water Longer Than a Driveway's Control Joints",
     "<p>A stamped pattern has more linear feet of grout lines than a plain slab has control joints, and once those lines fill with leaves, sand or mulch heading into the wetter months, they hold standing water against the surface longer than an open groove would, which in Florida's humidity is enough to start mold or algae growth in the low points of the pattern. Sweeping or rinsing a {city} patio's pattern lines clear before the rainy season gets going keeps that from taking hold. It is a simple habit, but one that is easy to skip on a surface that still looks clean everywhere except down in the grooves.</p>"),
    ("Rinsing Pool Splash Off a Stamped Deck Isn't Optional Upkeep",
     "<p>Chlorinated water splashed onto a stamped pool deck leaves behind more than a wet patch once it evaporates: repeated exposure without rinsing works on the sealer film the same way sun does, breaking it down faster right around the pool's edge than anywhere else on the same {city} deck. A quick hose-down of the areas that take the most splash, done as part of normal pool maintenance rather than as a separate task, slows that uneven wear enough to delay the next full reseal. Skipping it does not damage the concrete underneath, but it does mean the color and sheen around the pool fade noticeably faster than the rest of the deck.</p>"),
    ("Checking Overspray Against a New Stamped Surface, Not Just the Lawn",
     "<p>Watering rules differ across the service area right now, year-round limits on the Orlando side, while Sarasota and Manatee are currently under a stricter once-a-week schedule, but either way it is worth checking where a sprinkler head's spray actually lands once new landscaping or a new stamped driveway changes what used to be open yard. A head that drifts onto a {city} driveway's edge leaves mineral staining that shows up more against an integral color or hardener finish than it would on plain gray concrete, since the pattern makes any blotchy residue harder to ignore. A small aim adjustment after the schedule or the hardscape changes costs nothing.</p>"),
    ("A Hairline Crack Usually Follows a Grout Line First",
     "<p>On a stamped surface, ordinary shrinkage cracking tends to travel along the weakest point available, which is often a grout line already built into the pattern, rather than cutting a random path the way it might across plain concrete. A {city} homeowner checking a patio or driveway for cracks can start by looking along the pattern's existing lines before assuming a crack running elsewhere is something worse. What is worth a call, on a stamped surface the same as any other, is a sudden depression paired with cracking that reaches toward a structure, not a hairline that has been sitting quietly along a grout line for a season or two.</p>"),
    ("Why Shade From an Oak Can Leave a Patio Two-Toned",
     "<p>A stamped patio that sits partly under a live oak canopy and partly in open sun ages unevenly: the shaded section keeps its sealer sheen and color longer, while the exposed section fades from UV sooner, which over a few years can leave the same pour looking like two different colors side by side. On a {city} patio with this kind of mixed coverage, recoating the whole surface at once, rather than touching up just the faded section, keeps the sheen and tone consistent instead of adding a third shade to the mix. It is worth noting which parts of a patio sit under canopy before scheduling a reseal.</p>"),
    ("Matching Sheen, Not Just Color, When a Repair Gets Resealed",
     "<p>A patched section of a stamped surface, repaired after a chip or a flaking spot, needs more than the right color sealer to blend in: matte, satin and gloss sealers all catch light differently, and mixing sheens between an old, weathered section and a fresh patch makes the repair visible from across the yard even when the color is close. On a {city} job, matching the existing sheen level, worn down by however many seasons the rest of the surface has had, matters as much as matching the pigment. Skipping that step is how a careful color match still ends up looking like an obvious patch.</p>"),
  ],
}
